                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          %p Substantial work went into proving various casesy of the formula…
          %p
            A distinction aroise between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            of all algebraic
            = succeed "-bundles;" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_{\mathbb{C}}
          %p turned out to be immaterial for holomorphic sections (and higher cohomology of line bundles, which vanishes in both cases)
          %p
            But for more general vector bundles it became cleat that the Verline-style formulas apply to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            and not the space
          %p
            Recall that Narasimhan-Sechadri identity the moduli space of semi-stable algebraic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_{\mathbb{C}}
            bundles (modulo grade invariance)
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            of all holomorphic
            = succeed "-bundles" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_{\mathbb{C}}
          %p
            Has an atractive complex analytic presentation due to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Graeme+Segal"} Graeme Segal
          %p
            choose a disk
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta \subset \Sigma
            with smooth parameterized boundary
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial \Delta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}_G(\Sigma)
            is the double
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coset"} coset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hol
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
