          %p
            The symplectic structure here is independent of the complex structure omn
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
          %p
            The
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L G
            action is projective Hamiltonian, being a central extension lift to the prequantum line bundle
          %p
            So holomorphic and cohomological questions about the stack of all
            = succeed "-bundles" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_{\mathbb{C}}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            is equivalently
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hol
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hol(\Delta, G)
            \-equivariant holomorphic and cohomological on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo \
                      %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{\Sigma \backslash \Delta}
          %p
            Example: What is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo \
                      %mi Δ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^0(X_{\Sigma \backslash \Delta}, \mathcal{O}(k))
            as an
            = succeed "-representation?" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi dim
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dim H^0(K_G(\Sigma), \mathcal{O}(k))
          %p
            While complex analysis on such infinite-dimensional manifolds is still out of reach, there exists an algebraic model for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo \
                      %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{\Sigma \backslash \Delta}
            for which we can ask and answer analogous question
          %p
            Example:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo \
                      %mi Δ
                    %mi ab
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^{\gt 0}(X^{ab}_{\Sigma \backslash \Delta}, \mathcal{O}(k)) = 0
            “Kodeira vanishing”
          %p So we have an analytic index theorem for these varieties but without a topological side (this was “paradoxical”, because usually the topological side is easier.)
          %p
            But this requires finding a receptable fot the topological index (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann-Roch+theorem"}> Riemann-Roch theorem
            )
          %p
            Whet
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
            acts on R%X%, RR takes values in
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Rep
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒢
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi 𝒢
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi point
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi 𝒢
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi 𝒢
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi point
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Rep(\mathcal{G}) = K_{\mathcal{G}}(point) :
                  K_{\mathcal{G}}(X) \stackrel{p_*}{\to} K_{\mathcal{G}}(point)
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+integration"} fiber integration
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology+theory"} generalized cohomology theory
          %p
            Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                      %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi point
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_{L G}(point)
            is not “topological”, not got equivariant K-theory for non-compact groups!, so that map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_*
          %p But it turns out that both problems have a simultaneous solution.
          %p
            Instead consider
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                      %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_{L G}(\mathcal{A}_{S^1})
            for the gauge action of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L G
            inb tge space of flat
            = succeed "-connections" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            Instead of projecting
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo \
                      %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{\Sigma \backslash \Delta}
            to a point, use the flat connection model and restrict to the boundary
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∂
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo \
                      %mi Δ
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} o
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial \Delta : X_{\Sigma \backslash \Delta} \o \mathcal{A}_{\partial \Delta}
          %p
            This is a
            = succeed "!" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+map"} proper map
          %p
            The based loop group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega G
            acts freely and we can
          %p which leads to a well-defined map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi G
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  K_G(G^{2 g}) \stackrel{p_*}{\to} K_G(G)
          %p Amendments;
          %p
            we wanted projective representations of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L G
            with cocycle
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo ∈
                  %msubsup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                      %mi G
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ∼
                  %msubsup
                    %mi H
                    %mi G
                    %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  k \in H^2_{L G}(\mathcal{O}^\times)(\stackrel{\sim}{\to} H^3_G(G, \mathbb{Z}))
          %p
            So we should map to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+K-theory"} twisted K-theory
            group
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi K
                    %mi G
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  K_G^{k}(G)
          %p
            actually there is an extra shift by the dual Coxeter number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo =
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} = n
            for
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SU
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(n)
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor"} spinor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msqrt
                    %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sqrt K
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo \
                        %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{\Sigma \backslash \Delta}
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freed-Hopkins-Teleman+theorem"}> Freed-Hopkins-Teleman theorem
            \:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mi c
                  %msubsup
                    %mi K
                    %mi G
                    %mrow
                      %mi dim
                      %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  {}^{k + c}K_G^{dim G}(G)
          %p
            is a free abelian group generated by the positive energey irreps of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L G
            at level
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p generalization by Teleman and Woodward:
          %p
            analytical index = topological index for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo \
                      %mi Δ
                    %mi alg
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{\Sigma \backslash \Delta}^alg
          %p
            for essentially all K-theory clases, at least after inverting
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo +
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k+ c
          %p II. From loop group representations to K-theory
          %p Preparation: Compact groups:
          %p Recall the Kirillov correspondence between
          %p irreducilble representations and co-adjoint orbits (+ line bundles)
          %p In the connected case it can be summarized by saying that both of those correspond to the set of dominant integral regular wheights.
          %p
            Example:
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SU
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔰𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{su}(2) \simeq \mathbb{R}^3
          %p
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SO
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SO(3)
          %p but one can describe a canonical correspondence without directs reference to the classification of irreps
          %p
            Each
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coadjoint+orbit"} coadjoint orbit
            has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
            (see at
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbit+method"} orbit method
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ad
                    %mi ξ
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ad
                    %mi η
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi ξ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\omega_2(ad^*_\xi(\lambda), ad^*_\eta(\lambda))
                  \=
                  \\langle \lambda | [\xi,\eta]]\rangle
          %p
            For a vector bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            on the orbit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi O
                    %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O_\lambda
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_\lambda
            and which carries a lifted
            = succeed "-action." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            The Dirac index
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mi Ind
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi O
                    %mi λ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D Ind(O_\lambda, V)
            is a representation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            and this establishes the Kirillov correspondence
          %p
            Remarks: For connected
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            carries no information beyond its rank
          %p
            Wehn
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≠
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(G) \neq 0
            the
            = succeed "-action" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            should be projective and cancel the spinor projective cocycle
          %p The Dirac family consruction (Freed, Hopkins, Teleman) provides an inverse to this, assigning an orbit,…
          %p
            Input: irreducible representation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\lambda
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            highest weight
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            invariant metric on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
          %p
            Use Kostant’s “cubic Dirac operator” on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            for the metric connection with canonical torsion coming from
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^3(G, \mathbb{Z}) \simeq \mathbb{Z}
          %p Two key properties of this Dirac operator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ξ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ξ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [D, \psi(\xi)] = 2 T(\xi)
          %p (translation)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mo +
                  %mi p
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  D^2 = - (\lambda + p)^2
          %p
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi λ
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mo ±
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\lambda \otimes S^{\pm}
          %p
            The Dirac family is the
            = succeed "-graded" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}/2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mo ±
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \otimes S^{\pm}
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            and odd operator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi D
                    %mi ξ
                    %mi V
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi V
                  %mo +
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ξ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  D_\xi^V := D^V + \psi(\xi)
          %p
            Theorem (FHT): The kernel of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi D
                    %mi ξ
                    %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^V_\xi
            is supported on the orbit of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mo +
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda + p)
            and equals
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Kirillov
                  %mi line
                  %mi bundle
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Spinors
                  %mi to
                  %mi normal
                  %mi bundle
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Kirillov line bundle) \otimes (Spinors to normal bundle)
            In fact
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi D
                    %mi ξ
                    %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^V_\xi
            is a model for the Atiyah-Bott-Shapiro…
